RAWM.CONF(5)

# NAME

rawm.conf - rawm window-manager configuration

# DESCRIPTION

*rawm.conf* configures only state owned by *rawm*(1): window-manager
presentation, transient OSD feedback, client geometry policy, layouts, tags,
rules, and pointer operations that directly manipulate admitted clients.

Global keyboard bindings, argv execution, persistent panel/status presentation,
ALSA state, and system-tray ownership are not part of this schema. In a rawmkit
desktop those authorities normally belong to *rawkeys* and *rawpanel*.
Extracted settings fail validation instead of being accepted as inert
compatibility data.

The file uses libconfig syntax. Unknown settings, wrong types, invalid enum
values, out-of-range values, duplicate pointer bindings, and references to
nonexistent tags are rejected.

# FILES AND PRECEDENCE

Without *-c*, rawm starts with built-in defaults and then applies these layers:

. _/etc/rawm/rawm.conf_
. _$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawm.conf_ when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is an absolute path,
  otherwise _$HOME/.config/rawm/rawm.conf_

Missing normal system/user files are ignored. Existing files must be regular
files and must parse and validate successfully.

With *rawm -c PATH*, built-in defaults are followed by exactly _PATH_; the
normal system and user files are bypassed.

Use *rawm -C* to validate the effective configuration without opening X.

# LAYERING

Scalar and grouped settings override only members present in a later layer.
The following lists replace the corresponding earlier list when present:

*tags.default*
	The complete default tag list.

*tags.monitors*
	The complete per-monitor tag override list.

*rules*
	The complete rule list.

*pointer.bindings*
	The complete admitted-client pointer binding list.

# VALUE REFERENCES

String-valued *ui.font*, color components under *ui.colors*, and entries under
*layout.symbols* support *${path}* references after all layers have been read.
References may point to another reference-capable string or to these scalar
paths:

- *ui.border_width*
- *ui.snap*
- *ui.opacity*
- *ui.osd.show*
- *ui.osd.position*
- *ui.osd.geometry*
- *ui.osd.duration_ms*
- *ui.osd.padding*
- *ui.osd.offset*
- *ui.osd.min_width*
- *behavior.dialog_center*
- *behavior.size_hints*
- *behavior.floating_placement*
- *behavior.cascade_step*
- *layout.master_factor*
- *layout.master_count*
- *layout.gaps.inner*
- *layout.gaps.outer*
- *layout.gaps.smart*

References are recursive. Cycles, unknown paths, empty references, and
unterminated references are errors. Write *$${path}* to preserve the literal
text *${path}*.

# UI

The *ui* group accepts *font*, *border_width*, *snap*, *opacity*, *osd*, and
*colors*.

*font* = _string_
	Xft font pattern used by the transient OSD. It must not be empty. The default
	is *monospace:size=9*.

*border_width* = _integer_
	Managed-client border width in pixels, from 0 through 64. Default: 2.

*snap* = _integer_
	Pointer move/resize snap threshold in pixels, from 0 through 4096. Default:
	32.

*opacity* = _number_
	Client opacity projection from 0.0 through 1.0. Default: 1.0.

## OSD

The *ui.osd* group controls rawm's transient feedback surface. The OSD is the
only presentation surface currently owned by rawm and remains subject to a
separate authority decision.

*show* = _boolean_
	Enable transient OSD feedback. Default: false.

*position* = *"top"* | *"bottom"*
	Place the OSD relative to the selected monitor work area. Default: bottom.

*geometry* = _boolean_
	Show live geometry while pointer-moving or resizing floating clients.
	Default: false.

*duration_ms* = _integer_
	Visibility duration in milliseconds, from 100 through 5000. Default: 800.

*padding* = _integer_
	Inner OSD padding in pixels, from 0 through 64. Default: 10.

*offset* = _integer_
	Distance from the selected work-area edge, from 0 through 512. Default: 24.

*min_width* = _integer_
	Minimum OSD width in pixels, from 0 through 4096. Zero means no extra minimum.
	Default: 0.

## Colors

*ui.colors* accepts the schemes *normal*, *selected*, *urgent*, and *floating*. Each scheme may define:

*border* = _string_
	Border color.

*foreground* = _string_
	Text/foreground color.

*background* = _string_
	Background color.

The stock schemes are:

```
normal   = #cccccc / #000000 / #cccccc
selected = #0066ff / #ffffff / #0066ff
urgent   = #0066ff / #0066ff / #ffffff
floating = ${ui.colors.selected.*}
```

The tuple order above is border / foreground / background.

# BEHAVIOR

The *behavior* group accepts client-geometry policy.

*dialog_center* = *"none"* | *"monitor"* | *"parent"*
	Default centering policy for eligible floating/dialog clients. Default: none.
	Rules may override this per match.

*size_hints* = *"never"* | *"floating"* | *"always"*
	When ICCCM size hints constrain client geometry. Default: floating.

*floating_placement* = *"client"* | *"cascade"*
	Initial free-window placement policy. *client* preserves client-requested
	placement where possible; *cascade* uses rawm's monitor-local cascading
	placement. Default: cascade.

*cascade_step* = _integer_
	Cascade displacement in pixels, from 1 through 4096. Default: 32.

# LAYOUT

The *layout* group controls the tiled geometry model.

*master_factor* = _number_
	Master-area fraction, from 0.05 through 0.95. Default: 0.55.

*master_count* = _integer_
	Number of master clients, from 0 through 64. Default: 1.

## Gaps

*layout.gaps* accepts:

*inner* = _integer_
	Gap between tiled clients, from 0 through 4096. Default: 0.

*outer* = _integer_
	Gap between tiled clients and the monitor work-area edge, from 0 through 4096.
	Default: 0.

*smart* = _boolean_
	Suppress the outer gap when only one tiled client is present. Default: false.

## Symbols

*layout.symbols* may override the display symbol associated with each stable
layout name:

- *tile*
- *floating*
- *monocle*
- *bstack*
- *bstack_horizontal*
- *grid*

Each symbol is at most 32 bytes. Symbols are currently presentation labels
consumed by rawm's transient OSD.

# TAGS

The *tags* group accepts *default* and *monitors*.

*tags.default* is a list containing between 1 and 31 groups. Every entry
requires:

*name* = _string_
	Tag name, at most 22 bytes.

*layout* = _layout-name_
	Initial layout for the tag. Optional; default: tile.

Example:

```
tags = {
  default = (
    { name = "dev"; layout = "tile"; },
    { name = "web"; layout = "monocle"; }
  );
};
```

*tags.monitors* optionally replaces tag names/layout defaults for specific
monitor numbers. Each entry requires non-negative integer *monitor* and a *tags*
list using the same syntax as *tags.default*. Monitor numbers may not be
duplicated. Every monitor override must contain exactly the same number of tags
as the effective default tag list.

# RULES

*rules* is a list of groups. If present in a later configuration layer, it
replaces the complete earlier rule list.

A rule may match any combination of these substring fields:

*class*
	WM_CLASS class.

*instance*
	WM_CLASS instance.

*title*
	Current window title at admission.

*role*
	WM_WINDOW_ROLE.

Every specified matcher must match. Multiple matching rules are processed in
list order and may cumulatively modify the client.

A rule may set:

*tags* = _(integers)_ | *"all"*
	Tag numbers are one-based. Omitted means no explicit tag assignment.

*floating* = _boolean_
	Explicit floating state.

*center* = *"none"* | *"monitor"* | *"parent"*
	Per-rule centering policy.

*fullscreen* = _boolean_
	Initial fullscreen request.

*attention* = *"none"* | *"urgent"*
	Initial attention policy.

*monitor* = _integer_
	Target monitor number. *-1* means no explicit target; this is the internal
	default when the setting is omitted.

Example:

```
rules = (
  { class = "mpv"; tags = (8); fullscreen = true; },
  { role = "Progress"; floating = true; center = "monitor"; }
);
```

A configured tag number that does not exist in the effective tag list is a
validation error.

# POINTER

The *pointer* group is deliberately narrow. It configures only pointer actions
whose execution depends directly on admitted-client geometry and active pointer
grabs. It is not a generic input-binding facility.

*pointer.bindings* is a list. Every entry accepts:

*modifiers* = _(strings)_
	Optional modifier list. Valid names are *Shift*, *Control*, *Mod1*, *Mod2*,
	*Mod3*, *Mod4*, and *Mod5*.

*button* = _integer_
	Required X button number from 1 through 9.

*action* = _string_
	Required action. The only valid actions are *move*, *toggle_floating*, and
	*resize*.

The same modifier mask and button number may not appear twice.

The built-in bindings are:

```
pointer = {
  bindings = (
    { modifiers = ("Mod4"); button = 1; action = "move"; },
    { modifiers = ("Mod4"); button = 2; action = "toggle_floating"; },
    { modifiers = ("Mod4"); button = 3; action = "resize"; }
  );
};
```

There is no *click* selector: every binding targets an admitted client. Panel,
status, root-window, title-area, and layout-widget pointer policy belongs to the
owner of those surfaces.

# EXTRACTED SETTINGS

The following historical rawm settings are no longer accepted:

- *status*
- *commands*
- *input* (including the former *keys* and *buttons* members)
- *ui.bar*
- *ui.systray*

They are rejected as unknown settings. This is intentional fail-closed schema
contraction, not a deprecation alias. Configure global keys/argv execution in
*rawkeys* and persistent panel/status/systray policy in *rawpanel*.

# VALIDATION

*rawm -C* validates the effective configuration and exits with status 0 on
success. Validation does not open an X display.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawm.conf_
	System configuration.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawm.conf_
	User configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is absolute.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawm.conf_
	User fallback when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is unset, empty, or non-absolute.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm*(1), *rawctl*(1), *rawkeys*(1), *rawpanel*(1)

